<DocShell
   title="Recording sessions"
   summary="a11ied can record video of real VoiceOver and NVDA sessions, creating a visual evidence trail alongside the transcript and verification data."
   links={docsLinks}
>
   <section>
      <h2>Supported targets</h2>
      <ul>
         <li>Real VoiceOver sessions on macOS</li>
         <li>Real NVDA sessions on Windows</li>
         <li>Any command that starts or owns a real assistive technology session</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
         The virtual screen reader target does not support recording — it runs headlessly and
         produces transcript data instead.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2>Examples</h2>
      <pre><code>a1 drive start --target voiceover --recording ./recordings/voiceover-home.mov
a1 run pattern dialog_probe --target voiceover --url http://127.0.0.1:6173/dialog.html --recording ./recordings/dialog-probe.mov --json
a1 verify criterion 4.1.3 --target voiceover --url http://127.0.0.1:6173/status-message.html --recording ./recordings/status-message.mov --json</code></pre>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2>Setup with doctor</h2>
      <p>
         The <code>doctor</code> command checks whether your system can record. On macOS, recording
         failures are usually caused by missing Screen Recording permissions for the host app.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 doctor
a1 doctor --json</code></pre>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2>Troubleshooting</h2>
      <p>
         Run <code>doctor</code> first if recording fails. On macOS, a11ied records video without
         microphone input. If recording works from Terminal but fails from your editor or agent,
         check that the host app has Screen Recording permission in System Settings.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2>Privacy notes</h2>
      <ul>
         <li>Recordings may capture page content, system chrome, and assistive technology output.</li>
         <li>A recording documents one specific test run — include the artifact path in reports for traceability.</li>
      </ul>
   </section>

   <section class="field-note">
      <h2>Recordings as evidence</h2>
      <p>
         Video recordings supplement the transcript and verification data. They are useful for
         reviewing what the screen reader actually did, but the verification verdict comes from the
         <a href="/verification-semantics">verification</a> layer.
      </p>
   </section>
</DocShell>
